WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.000
The largest things in the&nbsp;
universe are black holes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.640
In contrast to things like planets or&nbsp;
stars they have no physical size limit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:14.240
and can literally grow endlessly. Although in&nbsp;
reality specific things need to happen to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:19.760
different kinds of black holes, from really tiny&nbsp;
ones to the largest single things in the universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:29.840
So how do black holes grow and how&nbsp;
large is the largest of them all?

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.520
This video will not discuss how black holes work&nbsp;
or how they form since we’ve looked at that in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:45.040
detail in our black hole and neutron star series,&nbsp;
you can check them out afterwards. For now we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.880
interested in finding the largest thing in the&nbsp;
universe. Let us start really, really small.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.120
Primordial Black holes

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:59.760
The smallest kind of black holes may or may not&nbsp;
exist. If they do, they are probably the oldest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:05.840
objects in the universe, older even than atoms.&nbsp;
They would have formed just after the big bang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.760
when the universe was so dense with&nbsp;
violent energy, that any tiny pocket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.760
that was just slightly more dense than&nbsp;
its neighbors could produce a black hole.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:18.480
The smallest Primordial Black Hole that&nbsp;
could still be around would be a trillion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:24.080
kilograms or so, the mass of a big mountain.&nbsp;
And yet they would be no bigger than a proton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.960
A Primordial Black hole with the mass of&nbsp;
earth would barely be larger than a coin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.480
This makes them very hard to find, so we&nbsp;
haven’t actually observed any yet – if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.840
they exist they may even be the mysterious&nbsp;
dark matter that holds galaxies together.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.960
Let’s move on to the kinds of black holes&nbsp;
that we know for sure ARE out there.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:46.720
Stellar Black Holes

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.240
To make a black hole we need to compress enough&nbsp;
matter so that it collapses into itself. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.440
that, the more mass we throw at it, the&nbsp;
larger it becomes. In today's universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.240
only the most violent cosmic events can create&nbsp;
the necessary conditions, such as the merger of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:10.080
neutron stars or when the core of a very massive&nbsp;
star collapses in a supernova. To have a unit to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:15.680
work with here, we’ll use the mass of our sun,&nbsp;
about 2 million trillion trillion kilograms.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.880
The smallest known black hole&nbsp;
has 2.7 times the mass of the sun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.080
which works out as a sphere around 16 km&nbsp;
in diameter, large enough to cover Paris.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.480
Another lightweight black hole is the&nbsp;
companion to the V723 Mon red giant star.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:39.200
This star is 24 times larger than our sun,&nbsp;
30 million kilometer in diameter. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:46.160
it is thrown around by a tiny black hole&nbsp;
just 17.2 km wide. This tiny thing bullying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.480
the star is so much smaller that we can&nbsp;
barely even show them in comparison.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:58.720
One of the largest known stellar black holes is&nbsp;
M33 X-7. It currently spends its time eating a 70&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.640
solar mass blue giant, bit by bit. As all that&nbsp;
stolen matter circles towards the black hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.120
like water going down a drain, friction heats&nbsp;
it up to temperatures high enough to shine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:17.200
500,000 times brighter than our Sun! And&nbsp;
yet, X-7 is ‘only’ 15.65 solar masses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.600
and 92 km wide, just big enough&nbsp;
to cast a shadow on Corsica.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.560
To grow much larger, black holes have to either&nbsp;
devour a lot of stars or better, merge with one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.080
another. The instruments that make it possible&nbsp;
to detect these mergers are very new so we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.840
currently discovering a lot of exciting things.&nbsp;
Like two massive black holes that we detected in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:46.080
a galaxy 17 billion lightyears away. As they spun&nbsp;
around each other violently, they released more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.520
energy in the form of gravitational waves than the&nbsp;
combined light from all the stars in the milky way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:04:00.480
in 4400 years. The new black hole they formed is&nbsp;
about the size of Germany and is 142 solar masses.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.160
And here we hit a curious gap in scale.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:10.800
There are lots of black holes up to 150 solar&nbsp;
masses. And then there is nothing for a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.560
time. Until we suddenly hit black holes,&nbsp;
that are millions of times more massive.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:21.680
Which is a bit confusing, because we had this&nbsp;
idea that black holes are consistently growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:27.520
and growing. But for the most massive black holes&nbsp;
this process is not fast enough to explain their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:32.400
existence today. The universe is simply not&nbsp;
old enough for these supermassive black holes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:37.120
to have formed by eating stars and merging with&nbsp;
each other. Something else must have happened.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.920
To explain how we got the largest black&nbsp;
holes in the universe, we might need the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:48.160
largest stars that ever existed: Quasi Stars.&nbsp;
To get a sense of scale, we can compare them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.920
to the largest stars that exist today. Our&nbsp;
Sun is like a grain of sand next to them.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:05:00.080
We don’t know if Quasi Stars actually existed but&nbsp;
they are an interesting concept when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:05.920
supercharging black hole development. The idea is&nbsp;
that the matter in the early universe was so dense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:11.280
that quasi stars could grow to thousands of&nbsp;
times the mass of our sun. The cores of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:16.480
stars might have been crushed by their own weight&nbsp;
so much to actually collapse into black holes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:21.840
while the star was still forming. In contrast to&nbsp;
stars today that would destroy themselves in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:27.840
process, inside quasi stars, a deadly balance&nbsp;
could emerge. Gravity pressed the supermassive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:32.720
star together, feeding the black hole and heating&nbsp;
the material falling in to such a degree that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:38.160
radiation pressure kept the star stable. And so&nbsp;
these quickly growing black holes might have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.600
able to consume the quasi star for millions of&nbsp;
years and grow far bigger than any modern stellar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:50.160
black hole. Black holes several thousand times the&nbsp;
mass of the Sun and wider than the entire earth.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:55.120
These black holes might have become&nbsp;
the seeds for supermassive black holes.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.640
Supermassive black holes

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:01.680
So now, we arrive at the kings of our&nbsp;
universe, the largest single bodies in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:08.480
existence. The centers of most galaxies contain a&nbsp;
super massive black hole, and they are monstrous.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:14.080
In the Milky Way we have Sagittarius A&nbsp;
Star, a super massive black hole with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:20.000
about 4 million solar masses that is calm and&nbsp;
collected and just does its thing. We know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:25.120
it sits there because we can see a number of stars&nbsp;
being thrown around by a seemingly empty spot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:29.440
And despite its incredible&nbsp;
mass, it’s radius is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:36.560
only 17 times our sun. Smaller than most giant&nbsp;
stars, but millions of times more massive.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.480
Because Supermassive black holes are so&nbsp;
massive and located at the center of galaxies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:47.120
many people imagine them as being a bit like&nbsp;
the Sun in the solar system. An anchor that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:53.440
glues everything else together and forces it into&nbsp;
an orbit. But this is a misconception. While the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:07:00.960
sun makes up 99.86% of all the mass in the solar&nbsp;
system, SuperMassive Black Holes usually only have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:08.560
0.001% of the mass of their galaxy. The billions&nbsp;
of stars in galaxies are not gravitationally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:13.520
bound to them, instead it is the gravitational&nbsp;
effect of dark matter which holds them together.

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:19.200
Many supermassive black holes aren’t gentle&nbsp;
giants, especially when they are feeding on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:25.360
the clouds of mass in their galaxy. The one at the&nbsp;
center of the BL Lacertae galaxy is devouring so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:29.840
much material that it produces jets of plasma&nbsp;
accelerated to nearly the speed of light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:37.120
If Earth were orbiting this huge body, it would&nbsp;
seem 115 times larger than our Sun in the sky…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:41.200
and we’d be burnt to a crisp in seconds&nbsp;
by its glowing hot accretion disk.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:47.600
At this point black holes become so large that&nbsp;
stars seem ridiculously tiny compared to them.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:57.040
The galaxy Cygnus A has a super massive black hole&nbsp;
with 2.5 billion solar masses and 14.7 billion km&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:01.760
wide, which would mean that if it took the place&nbsp;
of our Sun, it would swallow all the planets and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:08.320
stretch halfway to the edge of our Solar System.&nbsp;
It is devouring so much mass and material that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:14.640
churns its disk into a kind of magnetic funnel,&nbsp;
spewing gas out making tremendous radio lobes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:21.760
towering over the galaxy, half a million light&nbsp;
years in diameter. That is 2.5 Milkyways wide.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:28.400
Another pretty large Super Massive Black&nbsp;
hole sits in the galaxy Messier 87. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:34.160
has 6.5 billion solar masses and was the&nbsp;
first black hole we got an actual photo of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:38.800
Or rather of the glowing gas around&nbsp;
the edge of a menacing shadow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:44.800
This sphere of darkness is so large&nbsp;
that it covers our entire Solar System.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.600
And yet, there is a scale even&nbsp;
above these kinds of objects...

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.840
Ultramassive black holes

00:08:55.520 --> 00:09:01.040
Now we reach the most massive black holes, perhaps&nbsp;
the largest single bodies that will ever exist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:07.120
These black holes have eaten so much that they've&nbsp;
grown to tens of billions of solar masses, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:12.720
gravity the engine for a ‘quasar’- an accretion&nbsp;
disk shining brighter than thousands of galaxies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:18.800
full of stars. So massive that they deserve a&nbsp;
title of their own - Ultramassive Black Holes.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:27.040
The Ultra Massive Black Hole at the center of&nbsp;
galaxy OJ 287 is 18 billion solar masses. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:33.120
is so big that it has a Super Massive black hole,&nbsp;
nearly forty times larger than sagittarius A star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:38.560
orbiting it! This thing defies imagination&nbsp;
and is really hard to compare to anything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:43.680
It can comfortably fit three Solar&nbsp;
Systems side by side inside of it.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:49.040
Let us end this insane competition&nbsp;
and get to the king of kings.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:54.560
TON 618, a black hole that we can observe&nbsp;
consuming galaxies worth of matter is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:10:00.320
shining with the brightness of a hundred trillion&nbsp;
stars, visible from 18 billion light years away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:07.680
It has an incredible 66 billion solar masses.&nbsp;
A black hole so large that it would take light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:12.480
a week to reach the singularity after&nbsp;
crossing the event horizon. About 11&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:18.080
Solar Systems could sit inside of it side&nbsp;
by side. It may very well be the largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:22.480
single body in the universe. But in&nbsp;
reality, it is probably even larger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:28.800
Since TON 618 is so far away, we only see&nbsp;
what it looked like 10 billion years ago.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:33.760
In any case, black holes are scary and mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:40.400
and gigantic. They will be here after everything&nbsp;
else dies, and growing larger and larger.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:46.480
So now let us do the trip again. From&nbsp;
the smallest possible black hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:59.840
all the way up to the largest.

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.840
Let’s try something new today,&nbsp;
we can call it: “Behind the Lies”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:34.320
a short behind the scenes bit about the&nbsp;
necessary inaccuracies in this video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:38.880
because it's really not actually possible&nbsp;
to rank black holes like trading cards.

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:44.240
How so? Well, while we have catalogued&nbsp;
millions of stars, we really only have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:49.440
good data on a couple of dozen black holes.&nbsp;
That’s because black hole gazing wasn’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:54.400
a thing until 50 years ago – and technically&nbsp;
still isn’t, because we can’t see black holes.

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:58.640
We can only derive their properties from studying&nbsp;
their gravitational effects on the matter around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:03.680
them, like the orbit of stars that come close&nbsp;
to them. This effect depends on the mass of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:07.600
black hole, which we can approximate at&nbsp;
the most basic level with Kepler’s Laws.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:11.440
But this comes with huge&nbsp;
uncertainties and error bars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:16.080
Then we have to convert mass to size&nbsp;
next, which brings new uncertainties.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:21.040
For example, we calculated the radius from the&nbsp;
mass using the Schwarzschild equation which for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:26.560
the sake of simplicity assumes black holes are&nbsp;
perfectly round and don’t spin: a kind of black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:33.040
hole that doesn’t really exist. The reality is&nbsp;
that physics on these scales is a bit fuzzy.

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:37.600
So some of the black holes we talked about&nbsp;
here may be way smaller or way bigger. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:42.640
just don’t know for sure. We shimmied around&nbsp;
this problem by comparing different sources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:46.720
with different kinds of values and using&nbsp;
different mass calculations to arrive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:51.120
at a standardized list that allowed us&nbsp;
to be as accurate as humanly possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:56.320
You can look at all of this in our source doc. As&nbsp;
a result this script was written with the tears of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:00.960
experts we drove crazy with our obsession&nbsp;
for the best values they could live with.

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:05.120
In this process, tons of stuff&nbsp;
got cut and didn’t make it into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:08.880
the final video – but luckily we&nbsp;
found a way to not waste all of it:

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:16.080
We created a lot of black hole merch, spanning the&nbsp;
whole range from somewhat bonkers to more serious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:19.440
This way we get to explore a topic&nbsp;
from different angles – and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:33.840
get to continue having fun with&nbsp;
black holes after this video ends.

